@Article(time: 4) {
  @Intro(title: "Setup") {
    Rivulet is configured with environment variables. Only `DATABASE_URL` is required; every other
    variable has a default. A variable Rivulet does not read is silently ignored, so check the
    spelling.

    ```sh
    export DATABASE_URL=/var/lib/rivulet/rivulet.db
    export LISTEN_ADDR=0.0.0.0:8080
    export BASE_URL=https://reader.example.com
    export RUN_MIGRATIONS=1
    export POLLING_FREQUENCY=60
    export POLLING_PARSING_ERROR_LIMIT=3
    export LOG_LEVEL=info
    export LOG_FORMAT=text
    export FETCHER_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORKS=0
    ```

    `LISTEN_ADDR` must be `host:port`; the default is `127.0.0.1:8080`, and a value without a port is
    an error. `POLLING_FREQUENCY` is in minutes. Numeric settings have a minimum —
    `POLLING_FREQUENCY`, `BATCH_SIZE` and `WORKER_POOL_SIZE` at least 1, the two limits at least 0 —
    and a value below it is reported as an error rather than replaced by the default.

    `FETCHER_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORKS` stays off unless you subscribe to feeds on your own network: with
    it off, the fetcher refuses loopback and private addresses.

    The project README lists every variable with its default, including the fetcher settings
    (`HTTP_CLIENT_TIMEOUT`, `HTTP_CLIENT_MAX_BODY_SIZE`, `HTTP_CLIENT_USER_AGENT`,
    `HTTP_CLIENT_PROXY`, `FETCHER_IGNORE_HTTP_CACHE`) and the scheduler settings (`BATCH_SIZE`,
    `WORKER_POOL_SIZE`, `POLLING_LIMIT_PER_HOST`).

    `rivulet create-admin` and `rivulet integration-credentials` prompt for passwords on the
    terminal, so run them in an interactive shell, not from a service unit or a pipe.
  }
}
